## Intuition
In a binary tree, nodes at distance K from a target can lie in two directions — **downward** into the target's subtree, or **upward** through its ancestors. The downward direction is a straightforward DFS. The upward direction is the challenge: trees have no parent pointers, so we can't climb up directly.

The key insight is: the **root-to-target path** gives us every ancestor of the target, and the index of each ancestor in that path tells us exactly its hop-distance from the target. An ancestor at hop-distance `i` needs nodes at depth `k-i` in its subtree — but we must not go back down the direction we came from (toward the target). Instead of maintaining a visited set, we can simply pass the **previous path node as a blocker**, since each ancestor has exactly one child that lies on the path toward the target. Blocking that one child is sufficient.

## Approach

**Step 1 — Collect descendants of target:**
Run a DFS from the target node, collecting all nodes exactly `k` levels deep. These are all the "downward" answers.

**Step 2 — Build the root-to-target path:**
Use a recursive DFS with backtracking. Nodes are added to the list on the way down and removed on backtrack, so only the correct root→target path remains when the function returns.

**Step 3 — Search upward through ancestors:**
Reverse the path so `rootToTargetPath[0]` = target, `rootToTargetPath[1]` = parent, `rootToTargetPath[2]` = grandparent, and so on. For each ancestor at index `i` (hop-distance `i` from target), call a DFS searching for nodes at depth `k-i`, passing `rootToTargetPath[i-1]` as the blocked direction. This prevents re-entering the already-explored subtree toward the target without needing any extra data structure.

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {

    // Step 1: Collect all descendants of 'root' that are exactly k levels deep
    private void getChildAtDistanceK(TreeNode root, int k, List<Integer> childAns){
        if(root == null){
            return;
        }
        if(k == 0){
            childAns.add(root.data);
            return;
        }
        getChildAtDistanceK(root.left, k-1, childAns);
        getChildAtDistanceK(root.right, k-1, childAns);
    }

    // Step 3 helper: DFS from an ancestor, blocking the child direction
    // that leads back toward the target (already explored in Step 1)
    private void getNodesAtDistanceK(TreeNode root, TreeNode target, int k, List<Integer> NodeAns){
        if(root == null || root == target || k < 0){
            return;
        }
        if(k == 0){
            NodeAns.add(root.data);
            return;
        }
        getNodesAtDistanceK(root.left, target, k-1, NodeAns);
        getNodesAtDistanceK(root.right, target, k-1, NodeAns);
    }

    // Step 2: Build root→target path using DFS + backtracking
    // Nodes are added on the way down and removed on backtrack,
    // so only the correct root→target path remains in the list on return
    private boolean getRootToTargetPath(TreeNode root, TreeNode target, List<TreeNode> rootToTargetPath){
        if(root == null){
            return false;
        }
        rootToTargetPath.add(root);
        if(root == target){
            return true;                                      // target found, stop
        }
        boolean left = getRootToTargetPath(root.left, target, rootToTargetPath);
        if(left == true){
            return true;
        }
        boolean right = getRootToTargetPath(root.right, target, rootToTargetPath);
        if(right == true){
            return true;
        }
        rootToTargetPath.remove(rootToTargetPath.size()-1);  // backtrack
        return false;
    }

    public List<Integer> distanceK(TreeNode root, TreeNode target, int k) {
        List<Integer> answer = new ArrayList<>();
        if(root == null || target == null){
            return answer;
        }

        // Step 1: Collect all nodes at distance k going downward from target
        getChildAtDistanceK(target, k, answer);

        // Step 2: Build the root → target path
        List<TreeNode> rootToTargetPath = new ArrayList<>();
        boolean ifTargetPresent = getRootToTargetPath(root, target, rootToTargetPath);

        // Reverse so rootToTargetPath[i] = ancestor at hop-distance i from target
        // i.e. [0] = target, [1] = parent, [2] = grandparent, ...
        Collections.reverse(rootToTargetPath);

        int listSize = rootToTargetPath.size();

        // Step 3: For each ancestor at hop-distance i, find nodes at depth (k-i)
        // Pass rootToTargetPath[i-1] as blocked direction to avoid
        // re-exploring the subtree already covered in Step 1
        for(int i = 1; i < listSize; i++){
            if(k-i < 0){
                break;                                        // ancestor too far, no nodes possible
            }
            getNodesAtDistanceK(rootToTargetPath.get(i), rootToTargetPath.get(i-1), k-i, answer);
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity: O(N)**
`getRootToTargetPath` visits each node at most once — O(N). `getChildAtDistanceK` explores the target's subtree — O(N) worst case. The ancestor loop calls `getNodesAtDistanceK` for at most O(depth) ancestors, and across all those calls the total nodes visited is bounded by O(N) since the blocked-direction pruning ensures no node is processed more than once overall. Total: **O(N)**.

**Space Complexity: O(N)**
The recursion stack for `getRootToTargetPath` and `getChildAtDistanceK` is O(N) in the worst case (skewed tree). The `rootToTargetPath` list holds at most O(depth) = O(N) nodes. Notably, no parent map or visited set is allocated — the already-explored direction is blocked in O(1) by passing a single node reference, making this more space-efficient than the parent-map BFS approach which requires O(N) additional storage for the full tree.54:["$","$L7b",null,{"vi
